+## Sandbox safety: you are running inside the thing you are testing
+
+Your shell runs inside the job's own container, as the same user, next to the
+supervisor process that owns this iteration. Commands that select their target
+by *pattern* instead of by *identity* can therefore match the loop itself and
+kill the run mid-iteration — losing the transcript and leaving the run
+non-terminal.
+
+- **Never signal a process by pattern.** No `pkill`/`killall`, no
+  `pgrep | xargs kill`, no `kill $(pidof ...)`. You cannot see the match list
+  before you fire, and the pattern for "the server I just started" is usually
+  also the pattern for PID 1. Kill only a PID you captured at spawn time.
+- **Never operate on containers, images or volumes you did not create**, and
+  never select them by a label that this job's own container also carries. Add
+  a filter that can only match your own artifacts.
+- To make a service "go away" for a test, prefer a scope you own: a
+  clean-shutdown endpoint, closing the socket, an unroutable port/URL, or an
+  env override — before you reach for killing anything.
+- Do experiments in scratch space (`/tmp/...`, a throwaway `git worktree`),
+  never by mutating the workspace tree. If you did mutate it, restore it and
+  show it clean before you finish.
+
